for the record, here is the benchmark, time is measured by create some threads, acquire and release the spin lock for 1million times. values are in nanosecond, first value is average time, second is standard deviation. measurement is taken on my Loongson 3A6000 machine, using Linux 6.17.7-aosc-main-16k kernel, GCC 14.3.0

`nop` is an alias of `andi $r0, $r0, 0`, very fast, unsuitable for delay purpose. compared to `dbar`, `ibar` can block instruction fetching, which reduce contention and may save some power.

suggested upper bound is based on assumption of a single spin lock continuously being acquired by > 10 threads is not very realistic, so optimize for them is not very worthwhile.

suggested lower bound is based on minimize performance loss with 2 threads using as small as possible instructions.

